<<~ ahu slot=`#/findings`>>

!! Findings

* ''HIGH — Canary verdicts can select another rollout'' ([[launch-codex.mjs|/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/seat-harness/wt/tools/launch-codex.mjs:379]], [[launch-codex.mjs|/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/seat-harness/wt/tools/launch-codex.mjs:350]]): the launcher snapshots rollout paths //after// spawning Codex. If the canary rollout appears before that snapshot, it is recorded in `rollouts-before.json` and `--check-canary` excludes it. If multiple new rollouts share the seat cwd, `findRollout` chooses the earliest start, not necessarily the canary’s. The test writes a synthetic rollout after launch and never checks selection under either condition.

* ''MED — Check reports failure as success'' ([[launch-codex.mjs|/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/seat-harness/wt/tools/launch-codex.mjs:353]]): `--check-canary` returns 0 even when compaction did not fire; callers cannot use its exit status to gate on the claimed proof.

* ''MED — Canary run test would pass if the feature did nothing'' ([[launch-codex.test.mjs|/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/seat-harness/wt/tools/launch-codex.test.mjs:491]]): it doesn’t assert that a canary rollout is excluded from the prelaunch snapshot, nor that the returned rollout belongs to this launch. Its fixture manually creates a matching rollout after launch.

* ''LOW — Verdict call count misses tool variants'' ([[launch-codex.mjs|/home/joshu/.lares-scratch/seat-harness/wt/tools/launch-codex.mjs:146]]): only three payload types count, so the reported call total can undercount actual tool calls. The brief asks for ~100 calls, but the verdict never validates that count.

The `-c` overrides appear before the positional brief, and dry-run returns before the canary brief write. I found no defect in those points. The tests validate argv presence, not that Codex accepts these override keys or their placement.

<<~/ahu>>

